<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    The Fluent half of the Windows tray, and later the settings window. Deliberately not
    built by CMake: CMake cannot sensibly build a WinUI project, so this is a .csproj that
    `dotnet build` handles and CI invokes separately. The C++ solution stays C++, and the
    Windows App SDK never touches the daemon.

    Self-contained on purpose. The daemon runs from an unpacked tarball with nothing
    installed, and a menu that requires the user to go and find .NET 10 and the App SDK
    first would not be a menu they ever see. The size is paid by this program alone, and
    only by people who install it. dragonperch.exe neither knows nor cares whether it is
    there.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>WinExe</OutputType>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0-windows10.0.26100.0</TargetFramework>
    <!--
      Windows 11, not Windows 10. This program is the optional half: the daemon runs
      without it and falls back to a Win32 menu, so the floor here costs nobody their pets.
      What it buys is that everything Fluent 2 asks for can be assumed rather than guarded.
      Segoe UI Variable is the immediate one, since it is the type ramp and it arrived in
      22000; rounded corners, Mica and the WinUI 3 look generally are the same story.
    -->
    <TargetPlatformMinVersion>10.0.22000.0</TargetPlatformMinVersion>
    <RuntimeIdentifier>win-x64</RuntimeIdentifier>
    <Platforms>x64</Platforms>
    <AssemblyName>DragonPerch.Shell</AssemblyName>
    <RootNamespace>DragonPerch.Shell</RootNamespace>
    <UseWinUI>true</UseWinUI>
    <WindowsPackageType>None</WindowsPackageType>

    <!--
      Needed even though nothing here is packaged, and leaving it out is a silent bug
      rather than a build failure. It is what carries DragonPerch.Shell.pri the app's
      own resource index, holding the compiled App.xaml and with it the merged
      XamlControlsResources. `dotnet build` produces the .pri
      either way, so a locally built shell looks right; only `dotnet publish` drops it, and
      the only symptom is that every control falls back to its built-in template. The menu
      still works, still has the right items and icons, and is no longer Fluent: square
      corners, no shadow, a full-width separator. Nothing says so anywhere.
    -->
    <EnableMsixTooling>true</EnableMsixTooling>
    <WindowsAppSDKSelfContained>true</WindowsAppSDKSelfContained>
    <SelfContained>true</SelfContained>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <AllowUnsafeBlocks>true</AllowUnsafeBlocks>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
    <ApplicationIcon>..\..\packaging\dragonperch.ico</ApplicationIcon>

    <!--
      Native AOT, which turns out to suit this program unusually well. The managed side
      collapses from a CLR plus a 25 MB Windows SDK projection into one 4.6 MB executable,
      and a cold start goes from seconds to roughly 120 ms measured through the tray icon.
      That last number is the one that matters: this process exists to put a menu on the
      screen between somebody deciding to right-click and finishing the click.

      It also builds and runs, which is not a given for WinUI. What it does NOT do is
      change how the menu looks; that was checked pixel for pixel against a JIT build
      before the AOT was believed.
    -->
    <PublishAot>True</PublishAot>

  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(Configuration)|$(Platform)'=='Release|x64'">
    <DebugType>none</DebugType>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Not the Microsoft.WindowsAppSDK umbrella. That package is a metapackage over ten
    others, and four of them are things a tray menu has no use for: AI, ML, Search and
    Widgets. Together they are the Windows AI stack, and onnxruntime.dll plus DirectML.dll
    alone came to 38 MB.

    There is no property to switch them off; the umbrella offers none. Naming the two that
    are actually wanted is the supported way to say it, and it is safe because
    Microsoft.WindowsAppSDK.WinUI depends on Base, Foundation and InteractiveExperiences
    but on none of the four.

    These are the exact versions the 2.4.0 umbrella itself pins, read out of its nuspec, so
    this is the same code that a 2.4.0 reference would have given. They are stable
    releases: querying each package for its *newest* version returns an -experimental one
    and gives the opposite impression.
  -->
  <!--
    The Windows Community Toolkit's SettingsCard would be the obvious way to lay the
    settings window out, and was tried: it pins Microsoft.WindowsAppSDK 1.6, which collides
    with the 2.x packages above: duplicate imports and a hard error out of the MSIX build
    tools. Going back to the 1.6 umbrella to get it would cost the 38 MB of AI stack this
    project just finished removing. The settings page is built from WinUI's own controls
    and the system's own theme resources instead, which is where SettingsCard gets its look
    from anyway.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.WindowsAppSDK.WinUI" Version="2.3.6" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.WindowsAppSDK.Runtime" Version="2.4.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Note on the native linker's symbols, because they are 21 MB of an 82 MB publish
    directory and the obvious ways to stop that do not work: `NativeDebugSymbols=false` is
    ignored, and removing the file from ResolvedFileToPublish is ignored too, because AOT
    symbols are copied by the native-binary targets rather than through that list. A
    Delete after the Publish target is also undone.

    Left in place here, and dropped by CI when it builds the zip - which is arguably where
    it belongs: symbols are worth having next to a locally published build and are not
    worth 21 MB in something people download.
  -->

</Project>
